<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Ekiti Parapo</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1893 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An alliance of Ekiti, Ijesha and other Yoruba states that fought Ibadan to a standstill in the Kiriji war, one of the longest wars of 19th-century Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the 1850s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ibadan">Ibadan</a> dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekiti-people">Ekiti</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ijesha-kingdom">Ijesha</a> towns of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>, collecting tribute through resident agents. In 1877-1878 these towns rose in revolt and formed the Ekiti Parapo, meaning Ekiti together, joining forces with the Ijesha general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedengbe">Ogedengbe</a> and allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbomina">Igbomina</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The alliance had no single ruler; it was a military coalition of kings and war chiefs, led in the field by men such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedengbe">Ogedengbe</a> and Fabunmi of Oke-Mesi. Its soldiers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekiti-people">Ekiti</a> who had returned from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a>, used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breech-loading-rifle">breech-loading rifles</a> bought through the coast. The main armies faced <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ibadan">Ibadan</a> for years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a> at Kiriji, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbajo">Igbajo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Missionaries and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a> officials brokered a peace in 1886, but full settlement came only in 1893, when the Governor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagos-colony">Lagos Colony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-carter">Gilbert Carter</a>, imposed terms. Soon afterwards the area came under British rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1878 CE – 1893 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.07%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1893 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ogedengbe, Fabunmi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yoruba religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yoruba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ekitiparapo, Ekiti Confederacy</dd></dl></aside></div>

